---
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';

const updated = '2026-08-30';
---
<Layout
  title="Terms of use"
  description="How Oddversity content may be used, what it does not warrant, and the honest limits of technical material that ages."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'Terms', url: '/terms' }]}
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header>
      <span class="eyebrow">Terms of use</span>
      <h1>The reasonable expectations, both ways.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Short, because the site is simple: it publishes technical writing and asks
        nothing of you in return.
      </p>
      <p class="updated mono">Last updated {updated}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Using the content</h2>
      <p>
        Read it, learn from it, and use what you learn in your own work, commercial
        or otherwise, without attribution or permission. That is what it is for.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Code samples</strong> are provided so you can use them. Copy them
        into your projects freely. They are illustrative rather than production
        artefacts — read them before you run them, and adapt the error handling and
        limits to your own context.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Prose and diagrams</strong> remain the author's. Quote a paragraph
        with a link, by all means. Republishing pages wholesale, or feeding the
        catalogue into a derivative site, is not permitted — the citation graph is
        the only thing a free site has.
      </p>

      <h2>Accuracy, and its limits</h2>
      <p>
        This is technical material about a field that changes quickly. It is written
        carefully, cites primary sources where a claim is time-sensitive, and states
        its uncertainty where the evidence is thin. It will still contain errors, and
        some pages will be out of date before they are corrected.
      </p>
      <p>
        Verify anything you are about to depend on — particularly security guidance,
        cost figures and provider-specific behaviour, all of which move. Nothing here
        is professional, legal or security advice for your specific system.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you find an error, reporting it is genuinely the most useful thing you can
        do here. Name the page and the specific claim.
      </p>

      <h2>No warranty</h2>
      <p>
        The site and its content are provided as they are, without warranty of any
        kind. Nothing is guaranteed to be available, correct, current or fit for any
        particular purpose, and no liability is accepted for outcomes arising from
        acting on it.
      </p>

      <h2>Availability</h2>
      <p>
        The site is free and best-effort. There is no uptime commitment, no support
        obligation and no guarantee that any URL will exist forever — though URLs are
        treated as a contract worth keeping, and pages are redirected rather than
        deleted wherever that is possible.
      </p>

      <h2>Third-party names</h2>
      <p>
        Product, company and protocol names are used descriptively, to teach how the
        things they name actually behave. Their trademarks belong to their owners.
        Oddversity is independent and unaffiliated: where vendors differ, that
        difference is the content, and no coverage here is endorsed or sponsored.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        These terms may change; the date at the top will change with them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .wrap { max-width: 720px; margin-inline: auto; padding-block: 56px 8px; padding-inline: 32px; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(30px, 4.6vw, 42px); letter-spacing: -.022em; margin-top: 12px; }
  .lede { margin-top: 18px; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--muted); }
  .updated { margin-top: 14px; font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--muted); }
  .prose { margin-top: 36px; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .wrap { padding-block: 36px 8px; padding-inline: 20px; } }
</style>
